<blockquote>
<p>So why isn't it picking up huge amounts of energy from the rotation of the Earth</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's all part of the system. It's not a change in the system, because those values are constant for the Earth-object system. It's not a change in energy for its system, since we treat the Earth as our reference point.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also what about changes in gravitational energy as the moon orbits the Earth and the Earth orbits the Sun?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The gravitational energy from the Moon is so ridiculously tiny (using Newton's Law of Universal Gravitation for our g-constant) that it's essentially negligible.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Aren't there all sorts of particles that are sleeting through the Earth and aren't stopped by basically anything?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, but you have to remember that neutrinos are measured in terms of fractions of an electron volt (eV). 1 eV is equal to 1.60217657 × 10^-19 joules, which is roughly 600 quadrillion time the amount of energy it takes to raise a 10 gram ball 1 meter. That's ridiculously negligible.</p>

